<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-matrix-of-transformation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Matrix of a Linear Transformation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A linear transformation has no single matrix: it has one matrix per choice of basis
      for the domain and codomain. Changing those choices conjugates the matrix by a
      change of basis matrix, and a well-chosen basis can make the matrix strikingly
      simple.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-matrix-wrt-bases">
    <title>Four Matrices for One Transformation</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-four-matrices">
      <title>The Same Transformation in Different Bases</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> be the linear transformation given by
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -5x + 6y \\ -3x + 4y \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          and let
          <m>\mathcal{B} = \{ \vec{e}_1, \vec{e}_2 \}
          = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>
          and
          <m>\mathcal{B}' = \{ \vec{b}_1, \vec{b}_2 \}
          = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>
          be the bases.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="I">
            <li><p>Compute the matrix <m>A_1</m> of <m>T</m> with respect to <m>\mathcal{B}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute the matrix <m>A_2</m> of <m>T</m> with respect to <m>\mathcal{B}'</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the matrix <m>A_3</m> of <m>T</m> if the bases <m>\mathcal{B}</m> and
                <m>\mathcal{B}'</m> are used for domain and codomain respectively.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the matrix <m>A_4</m> of <m>T</m> if the bases <m>\mathcal{B}'</m> and
                <m>\mathcal{B}</m> are used for domain and codomain respectively.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First note the relations between the two bases, which we will need throughout:
          <md>
            \vec{e}_1 = -\vec{b}_1 + \vec{b}_2,
            \qquad
            \vec{e}_2 = 2\vec{b}_1 - \vec{b}_2,
          </md>
          so that
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}} = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            P_{\mathcal{B} \leftarrow \mathcal{B}'} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(I)</term> Since
          <m>T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -5 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}</m>
          and
          <m>T \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 6 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <md>
            A_1 = \begin{bmatrix} -5 \amp 6 \\ -3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(II)</term> We compute the images of the new basis vectors and express
          them in the new basis:
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \quad \text{and} \quad
            \left[ \begin{matrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{matrix} \right]_{\mathcal{B}'}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -4 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}
            \quad \text{and} \quad
            \left[ \begin{matrix} -4 \\ -2 \end{matrix} \right]_{\mathcal{B}'}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          which gives
          <md>
            A_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Alternatively, starting from
          <m>A_1 [\vec{u}]_{\mathcal{B}} = [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{B}}</m> and inserting
          <m>P^{-1}P = I</m>,
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}} \, A_1 \,
            \underbrace{P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}}^{-1}
            P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}}}_{I} [\vec{u}]_{\mathcal{B}}
            = P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}} [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{B}},
          </md>
          i.e., <m>A_2 [\vec{u}]_{\mathcal{B}'} = [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{B}'}</m> where
          <md>
            A_2 = P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}} \, A_1 \,
                  P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}}^{-1}
                = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} -5 \amp 6 \\ -3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}. \checkmark
          </md>
          Note how much simpler <m>A_2</m> is than <m>A_1</m>: in the basis
          <m>\mathcal{B}'</m> the transformation just scales <m>\vec{b}_1</m> by <m>1</m>
          and <m>\vec{b}_2</m> by <m>-2</m>. Indeed
          <md>
            T \left( x\vec{b}_1 + y\vec{b}_2 \right)
            = x \, T(\vec{b}_1) + y \, T(\vec{b}_2)
            = x\vec{b}_1 - 2y\vec{b}_2.
          </md>
          This is exactly the phenomenon that
          <xref ref="ch-eigenvalues" text="type-global" /> is about.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(III) First method.</term> Express the images of the <m>\mathcal{B}</m>
          basis vectors in the <m>\mathcal{B}'</m> basis:
          <md>
            \left[ T(\vec{e}_1) \right]_{\mathcal{B}'}
            = \left[ \begin{matrix} -5 \\ -3 \end{matrix} \right]_{\mathcal{B}'}
            = -1 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} - 2 \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \; \longrightarrow \; \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          <md>
            \left[ T(\vec{e}_2) \right]_{\mathcal{B}'}
            = \left[ \begin{matrix} 6 \\ 4 \end{matrix} \right]_{\mathcal{B}'}
            = 2 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} + 2 \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \; \longrightarrow \; \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          so
          <m>A_3 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ -2 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(III) Second method.</term> From
          <m>A_1 [\vec{u}]_{\mathcal{B}} = [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{B}}</m>, multiply both
          sides by <m>P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}}</m>:
          <md>
            A_3 = P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}} \, A_1
                = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} -5 \amp 6 \\ -3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ -2 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}. \checkmark
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(IV) First method.</term>
          <md>
            \left[ T(\vec{b}_1) \right]_{\mathcal{B}} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \left[ T(\vec{b}_2) \right]_{\mathcal{B}} = \begin{bmatrix} -4 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}
            \qquad \Longrightarrow \qquad
            A_4 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -4 \\ 1 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(IV) Second method.</term>
          <md>
            A_4 = A_1 \, P_{\mathcal{B} \leftarrow \mathcal{B}'}
                = \begin{bmatrix} -5 \amp 6 \\ -3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -4 \\ 1 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          or equally
          <md>
            A_4 = P_{\mathcal{B} \leftarrow \mathcal{B}'} \, A_2
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -4 \\ 1 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}. \checkmark
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-four-matrices-p2">
      <title>The Same Game in <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \mathbb{P}_2 \to \mathbb{P}_2</m> be the linear transformation
          <md>
            T\left( p(x) \right) = p(x) + p'(x),
          </md>
          and consider the ordered bases
          <m>\mathcal{B}_1 = \{ 1, x, x^2 \}</m> and
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2 = \{ 1, \; 1 + x, \; 1 + x + x^2 \}</m> of
          <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="I">
            <li><p>Compute the matrix <m>A_1</m> of <m>T</m> with respect to <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute the matrix <m>A_2</m> of <m>T</m> with respect to <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the matrix <m>A_3</m> of <m>T</m> if the bases <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>
                and <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> are used for domain and codomain respectively.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the matrix <m>A_4</m> of <m>T</m> if the bases <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>
                and <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> are used for domain and codomain respectively.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Unlike the previous example, the vectors here are polynomials, not columns.
          The coordinate maps are the bridge: they are isomorphisms
          <m>\mathbb{P}_2 \to \R^3</m>, and once every polynomial is replaced by its
          coordinate vector, all four computations become the same matrix arithmetic in
          <m>\R^3</m> as before.
        </p>
        <p>
          The strategy is captured by the following diagram. A basis chosen for the domain
          identifies <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> with <m>\R^3</m>, and so does a basis chosen for the
          codomain. Through these two coordinate maps the transformation <m>T</m> corresponds
          to a matrix transformation <m>\R^3 \to \R^3</m>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-p2-four-matrices-square">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>T : \mathbb{P}_2 \to \mathbb{P}_2</m> and the matrix
            transformation <m>\R^3 \to \R^3</m> induced by the coordinate maps of the two
            chosen bases.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-p2-four-matrices-square" width="85%">
            <shortdescription>A commutative square: T maps P2 to P2 across the top, a matrix A maps R3 to R3 across the bottom, and the coordinate maps of the domain basis and of the codomain basis join top to bottom.</shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pf-p2-four-matrices-square">
              <diagram dimensions="(400,196)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="[0,0,400,196]">
                  <group at="square">
                    <label at="node-tl" anchor="(62,164)" alignment="center" scale="1.2"><m>\mathbb{P}_2</m></label>
                    <label at="node-tr" anchor="(338,164)" alignment="center" scale="1.2"><m>\mathbb{P}_2</m></label>
                    <label at="node-bl" anchor="(62,36)" alignment="center" scale="1.2"><m>\mathbb{R}^3</m></label>
                    <label at="node-br" anchor="(338,36)" alignment="center" scale="1.2"><m>\mathbb{R}^3</m></label>
                    <group at="arrow-top">
                      <line endpoints="[(92,164),(302,164)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(197,170)" alignment="north" scale="1.1"><m>T</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-left">
                      <line endpoints="[(62,144),(62,56)]" arrows="2" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(69,100)" alignment="east" scale="0.9"><m>\text{domain basis}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-right">
                      <line endpoints="[(338,144),(338,56)]" arrows="2" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(331,100)" alignment="west" scale="0.9"><m>\text{codomain basis}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-bottom">
                      <line endpoints="[(92,36),(308,36)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(200,42)" alignment="north" scale="1.1"><m>A</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(200,29)" alignment="south" scale="0.8"><m>\text{matrix transformation}</m></label>
                    </group>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="square" text="A commutative square relating the transformation T on P 2 to a matrix transformation on R 3">
                    <annotation ref="arrow-top" text="Across the top, T maps the polynomial space P 2 to itself"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-left" text="On the left, the coordinate map of the basis chosen for the domain identifies P 2 with R 3, in both directions"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-right" text="On the right, the coordinate map of the basis chosen for the codomain identifies P 2 with R 3, in both directions"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-bottom" text="Across the bottom, multiplication by the matrix A maps R 3 to R 3; A is one of A 1, A 2, A 3, A 4, depending on the two bases"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The four matrices of this example are the four ways of choosing the two bases:
        </p>

        <tabular halign="center">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor"><cell>Matrix</cell><cell>Basis for the domain</cell><cell>Basis for the codomain</cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>A_1</m></cell><cell><m>\mathcal{B}_1</m></cell><cell><m>\mathcal{B}_1</m></cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>A_2</m></cell><cell><m>\mathcal{B}_2</m></cell><cell><m>\mathcal{B}_2</m></cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>A_3</m></cell><cell><m>\mathcal{B}_1</m></cell><cell><m>\mathcal{B}_2</m></cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>A_4</m></cell><cell><m>\mathcal{B}_2</m></cell><cell><m>\mathcal{B}_1</m></cell></row>
        </tabular>

        <p>
          In every case the square says the same thing: taking coordinates and then
          multiplying by the matrix gives the same result as applying <m>T</m> and then
          taking coordinates. For <m>A_3</m>, for instance,
          <m>A_3 [\, p \,]_{\mathcal{B}_1} = [\, T(p) \,]_{\mathcal{B}_2}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          First the change-of-basis matrices, exactly as in
          <xref ref="eg-p2-change-of-basis" text="type-global" />: reading the elements
          of <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> in the basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> columnwise, and
          inverting,
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}_1 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_2}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(I)</term> Transform the basis elements of <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> and read
          the outputs in <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>:
          <md>
            T(1) = 1, \qquad
            T(x) = x + 1, \qquad
            T(x^2) = x^2 + 2x,
          </md>
          whose coordinate vectors form the columns
          <md>
            A_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(II) First method.</term> Transform the basis elements of
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> and express the outputs in <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              T(1) \amp = 1 = 1(1) \; \longrightarrow \; (1, 0, 0),
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              T(1 + x) \amp = 2 + x = 1(1) + 1(1 + x) \; \longrightarrow \; (1, 1, 0),
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              T(1 + x + x^2) \amp = 2 + 3x + x^2
              = -1(1) + 2(1 + x) + 1(1 + x + x^2) \; \longrightarrow \; (-1, 2, 1),
            </mrow>
          </md>
          so
          <md>
            A_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          At the level of the ordered basis, this computation is the following diagram.
          Along the top, <m>T</m> sends each element of <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> to its image.
          The coordinate maps turn the elements of <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> into the standard
          basis columns of <m>\R^3</m> and the images into their
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>-coordinate vectors, and <m>A_2</m> must carry the former to
          the latter. That is why the coordinate vectors of the images are the columns of
          <m>A_2</m>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-p2-four-matrices-basis">
          <caption>
            The action of <m>T</m> on the ordered basis <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> and the
            corresponding matrix <m>A_2</m>. One colour marks a basis element, its image
            and their coordinate vectors.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-p2-four-matrices-basis" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>A square in which T carries 1, 1 + x, 1 + x + x squared to 1, 2 + x, 2 + 3x + x squared, while A2 carries the standard basis columns of R3 to the columns (1,0,0), (1,1,0), (-1,2,1).</shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pf-p2-four-matrices-basis">
              <diagram dimensions="(436,167)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="[0,0,436,167]">
                  <group at="square">
                    <group at="top-left">
                      <label anchor="(20,131)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(62,131)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>1+x</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(128,131)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>1+x+x^2</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="top-right">
                      <label anchor="(276,131)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(318,131)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>2+x</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(388,131)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>2+3x+x^2</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="bottom-left">
                      <polygon points="((16,70),(12,70),(12,12),(16,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((24,70),(28,70),(28,12),(24,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(20,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(20,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(20,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((58,70),(54,70),(54,12),(58,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((66,70),(70,70),(70,12),(66,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(62,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(62,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(62,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((124,70),(120,70),(120,12),(124,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((132,70),(136,70),(136,12),(132,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(128,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(128,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(128,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>1</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="bottom-right">
                      <polygon points="((272,70),(268,70),(268,12),(272,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((280,70),(284,70),(284,12),(280,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(276,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(276,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(276,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((314,70),(310,70),(310,12),(314,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((322,70),(326,70),(326,12),(322,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(318,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(318,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(318,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((377,70),(373,70),(373,12),(377,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((399,70),(403,70),(403,12),(399,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(388,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>-1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(388,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>2</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(388,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>1</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="map-left">
                      <line endpoints="[(20,117),(20,76)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(62,117),(62,76)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(128,117),(128,76)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <label anchor="(167,96.5)" alignment="east" scale="0.8"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\mathcal{B}_2}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="map-right">
                      <line endpoints="[(276,117),(276,76)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(318,117),(318,76)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(388,117),(388,76)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <label anchor="(261,96.5)" alignment="west" scale="0.8"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\mathcal{B}_2}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-top">
                      <line endpoints="[(174,131),(254,131)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(211,137)" alignment="north" scale="1"><m>T</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-bottom">
                      <line endpoints="[(174,41),(254,41)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(211,47)" alignment="north" scale="1"><m>A_2</m></label>
                    </group>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="square" text="A square showing where T sends each element of the basis B 2 and, underneath, the same thing in B 2 coordinates">
                    <annotation ref="top-left" text="Top left: the three elements of the basis B 2, which are 1, 1 plus x, and 1 plus x plus x squared"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-top" text="An arrow labelled T from the basis elements to their images"/>
                    <annotation ref="top-right" text="Top right: their images under T, in the same order: 1, 2 plus x, and 2 plus 3 x plus x squared"/>
                    <annotation ref="map-left" text="Double-headed arrows join each basis element to its B 2 coordinate vector below it"/>
                    <annotation ref="bottom-left" text="Bottom left: the coordinate vectors of the basis elements, which are the standard basis columns 1 0 0, then 0 1 0, then 0 0 1"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-bottom" text="An arrow labelled A 2 from the standard basis columns to the columns on the right"/>
                    <annotation ref="bottom-right" text="Bottom right: the B 2 coordinate vectors of the images, 1 0 0, then 1 1 0, then negative 1, 2, 1; these are the three columns of A 2"/>
                    <annotation ref="map-right" text="Double-headed arrows join each image to its B 2 coordinate vector below it"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          For the other three matrices only the labels change: the top left holds the
          basis of the domain, and the images are read in the basis of the codomain.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(II) Second method.</term> Exactly as in the previous example,
          <md>
            A_2 = P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1} \, A_1 \,
                  P_{\mathcal{B}_1 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_2}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}. \checkmark
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(III)</term> Multiplying
          <m>A_1 [\, p \,]_{\mathcal{B}_1} = [\, T(p) \,]_{\mathcal{B}_1}</m> on the
          left by <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m> converts the
          output coordinates,
          <md>
            A_3 = P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1} \, A_1
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          As a spot check on the second column: <m>T(x) = 1 + x</m> is itself the second
          element of <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>, so its <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>-coordinates are
          <m>(0, 1, 0)</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(IV)</term> This time the input coordinates arrive in
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> and must be converted before <m>A_1</m> can act:
          <md>
            A_4 = A_1 \, P_{\mathcal{B}_1 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_2}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          As a spot check on the third column:
          <m>T(1 + x + x^2) = 2 + 3x + x^2</m>, whose
          <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>-coordinates are <m>(2, 3, 1)</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-transformation-between-spaces">
    <title>A Transformation between Different Vector Spaces</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-p2-to-m22">
      <title>From <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> to <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> be the space of all <m>2 \times 2</m> matrices and
          let <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> be the space of all polynomials of at most degree
          <m>2</m>. Define <m>S : \mathbb{P}_2 \to \mathbb{M}_{22}</m> as the
          transformation given by
          <md>
            S \left( a_2 x^2 + a_1 x + a_0 \right)
            = \begin{bmatrix} a_1 + a_2 \amp a_2 + a_0 \\ a_1 - a_0 \amp a_1 + a_0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          for all <m>a_2 x^2 + a_1 x + a_0 \in \mathbb{P}_2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Describe the null space of <m>S</m>. Is <m>S</m> one-to-one?</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Express the range of <m>S</m> as a span of a linearly independent set of
                <m>2 \times 2</m> matrices. Is <m>S</m> onto?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Is <m>S</m> an isomorphism?</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Extend the basis of the range of <m>S</m> such that the extended basis,
                <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>, spans <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Consider the ordered basis <m>\mathcal{B} = \{ 1, x, x^2 \}</m> and the
                ordered basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> for <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> and find the
                matrix of the transformation <m>S</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Consider the ordered basis <m>\mathcal{B} = \{ 1, x, x^2 \}</m> and the
                ordered basis
                <md>
                  \mathcal{B}_2 = \left\{
                    \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
                    \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
                    \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
                    \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} \right\}
                </md>
                for <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> and find the matrix of the transformation
                <m>S</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(1)</term>
          <md>
            \operatorname{Nul} S = \left\{ P(x) = a_2 x^2 + a_1 x + a_0
            \; : \; S(P(x)) = 0_{2 \times 2} \right\}.
          </md>
          Setting
          <m>S(P(x)) = \begin{bmatrix} a_1 + a_2 \amp a_2 + a_0 \\ a_1 - a_0 \amp a_1 + a_0 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> gives
          <md>
            a_1 + a_2 = 0, \quad a_2 + a_0 = 0, \quad a_1 - a_0 = 0, \quad a_1 + a_0 = 0,
          </md>
          and the last two force <m>a_1 = a_0 = 0</m>, whence <m>a_2 = 0</m>. This is the
          trivial solution.
        </p>
        <p>
          Hence the null space only contains the zero vector of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>, which
          is known as the zero polynomial
          <m>P_0(x) = (0)x^2 + 0(x) + 0(1) = 0</m>, and <m>S</m> is one-to-one:
          <md>
            \operatorname{Nul} S = \{ P_0(x) \}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(2)</term> The output of <m>S</m> is
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} a_1 + a_2 \amp a_2 + a_0 \\ a_1 - a_0 \amp a_1 + a_0 \end{bmatrix}
            = a_0 \underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}}_{M_0}
            + a_1 \underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}}_{M_1}
            + a_2 \underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}}_{M_2}.
          </md>
          We can check that <m>\{ M_0, M_1, M_2 \}</m> is a linearly independent set and
          hence this set is a basis for the range of <m>S</m>, since the range of <m>S</m>
          is <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ M_0, M_1, M_2 \}</m>. This means
          <m>\dim (\text{range of } S) = 3</m>, whereas
          <m>\dim (\mathbb{M}_{22}) = 4</m>. Therefore <m>S</m> is NOT onto.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(3)</term> No. <m>S</m> is not onto.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(4)</term> We want to add a fourth matrix, <m>M_3</m>, such that
          <m>M_3</m> cannot be written as
          <m>M_3 = a_0 M_0 + a_1 M_1 + a_2 M_2</m>. In other words, writing
          <m>M_3 = \begin{bmatrix} b_1 \amp b_2 \\ b_3 \amp b_4 \end{bmatrix}</m>, the
          following must NOT have a solution:
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} b_1 \amp b_2 \\ b_3 \amp b_4 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} a_1 + a_2 \amp a_2 + a_0 \\ a_1 - a_0 \amp a_1 + a_0 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          As a system of linear equations in <m>a_2, a_1, a_0</m> we have
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp b_1 \\
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp b_2 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp b_3 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp b_4
            \end{array} \right]
            \longrightarrow \cdots \longrightarrow
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp b_1 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp b_4 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp b_3 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp b_3 + b_2 - b_1
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
          If <m>b_3 + b_2 - b_1 \neq 0</m>, then the system is inconsistent. For example,
          choose <m>b_1 = 1</m>, <m>b_2 = b_3 = b_4 = 0</m>, which gives
          <m>M_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore, the set
          <md>
            \mathcal{B}_1 = \{ M_0, M_1, M_2, M_3 \} = \left\{
              \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} \right\}
          </md>
          is a basis for <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(5)</term> The strategy is captured by the following diagram. The
          coordinate maps identify <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> with <m>\R^3</m> and
          <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> with <m>\R^4</m>, so the transformation <m>S</m>
          corresponds to a matrix transformation <m>\R^3 \to \R^4</m>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-commutative-diagram-S">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>S : \mathbb{P}_2 \to \mathbb{M}_{22}</m>
            and the matrix transformation <m>\R^3 \to \R^4</m> induced by the
            coordinate isomorphisms.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-commutative-diagram-S" width="62%">
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="commutative-diagram-S">
              <diagram dimensions="(460,280)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="[0,0,10,6]">
                  <group at="commutative-square">
                    <label at="node-P2" anchor="(1.5,5)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\mathbb{P}_2</m></label>
                    <label at="node-M22" anchor="(8.5,5)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m></label>
                    <label at="node-R3" anchor="(1.5,1)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\mathbb{R}^3</m></label>
                    <label at="node-R4" anchor="(8.5,1)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\mathbb{R}^4</m></label>

                    <line at="arrow-S" endpoints="[(2.4,5),(7.3,5)]" arrows="1"/>
                    <label at="label-S" anchor="(4.85,5.3)" alignment="north" scale="1.4"><m>S</m></label>

                    <line at="arrow-left-iso" endpoints="[(1.5,4.3),(1.5,1.7)]" arrows="2"/>
                    <line at="arrow-right-iso" endpoints="[(8.5,4.3),(8.5,1.7)]" arrows="2"/>

                    <line at="arrow-A" endpoints="[(2.2,1),(7.6,1)]" arrows="1"/>
                    <label at="label-A" anchor="(4.9,0.35)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\text{matrix transformation}</m></label>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="commutative-square"
                              text="A commutative square relating the transformation S to a matrix transformation">
                    <annotation ref="arrow-S" text="Across the top, S maps the polynomial space P 2 to the space of 2 by 2 matrices M 2 2"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-left-iso" text="On the left, the coordinate isomorphism identifies P 2 with R 3 in both directions"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-right-iso" text="On the right, the coordinate isomorphism identifies M 2 2 with R 4 in both directions"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-A" text="Across the bottom, a matrix transformation maps R 3 to R 4"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          At the level of the ordered bases, <m>S</m> sends the three elements
          <m>1, x, x^2</m> of <m>\mathcal{B}</m> to <m>M_0, M_1, M_2</m>, the first three
          elements of <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>. The coordinate maps turn <m>1, x, x^2</m> into
          the standard basis columns of <m>\R^3</m> and <m>M_0, M_1, M_2</m> into the first
          three standard basis columns of <m>\R^4</m>, and the induced matrix
          transformation, given by a matrix <m>A_1</m>, must carry the former to the latter:
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-basis-diagram-S">
          <caption>
            The action of <m>S</m> on the ordered basis <m>\mathcal{B}</m> and the
            corresponding matrix <m>A_1</m>. One colour marks a basis element, its image
            and their coordinate vectors.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-basis-diagram-S" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>A square in which S carries 1, x, x squared to the matrices M0, M1, M2, while A1 carries the standard basis columns of R3 to the first three standard basis columns of R4.</shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="basis-diagram-S">
              <diagram dimensions="(402,185)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="[0,0,402,185]">
                  <group at="square">
                    <group at="top-left">
                      <label anchor="(20,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(63,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>x</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(106,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>x^2</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="top-right">
                      <label anchor="(276,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>M_0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(327,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>M_1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(378,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>M_2</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="bottom-left">
                      <polygon points="((16,79),(12,79),(12,21),(16,21))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((24,79),(28,79),(28,21),(24,21))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(20,68)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(20,50)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(20,32)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((59,79),(55,79),(55,21),(59,21))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((67,79),(71,79),(71,21),(67,21))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(63,68)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(63,50)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(63,32)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((102,79),(98,79),(98,21),(102,21))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((110,79),(114,79),(114,21),(110,21))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(106,68)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(106,50)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(106,32)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>1</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="bottom-right">
                      <polygon points="((272,88),(268,88),(268,12),(272,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((280,88),(284,88),(284,12),(280,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(276,77)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(276,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(276,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(276,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((323,88),(319,88),(319,12),(323,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((331,88),(335,88),(335,12),(331,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(327,77)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(327,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(327,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(327,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((374,88),(370,88),(370,12),(374,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((382,88),(386,88),(386,12),(382,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(378,77)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(378,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(378,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(378,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="map-left">
                      <line endpoints="[(20,135),(20,85)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(63,135),(63,85)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(106,135),(106,85)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <label anchor="(121,114.5)" alignment="east" scale="0.8"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\mathcal{B}}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="map-right">
                      <line endpoints="[(276,135),(276,94)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(327,135),(327,94)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(378,135),(378,94)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <label anchor="(257,114.5)" alignment="west" scale="0.8"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\mathcal{B}_1}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-top">
                      <line endpoints="[(128,149),(250,149)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(186,155)" alignment="north" scale="1"><m>S</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-bottom">
                      <line endpoints="[(128,50),(250,50)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(186,56)" alignment="north" scale="1"><m>A_1</m></label>
                    </group>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="square" text="A square showing where S sends each element of the basis 1, x, x squared and, underneath, the same thing in coordinates">
                    <annotation ref="top-left" text="Top left: the three elements 1, x, x squared of the ordered basis B of P 2"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-top" text="An arrow labelled S from the basis elements to their images"/>
                    <annotation ref="top-right" text="Top right: their images under S, in the same order: the matrices M 0, M 1, M 2"/>
                    <annotation ref="map-left" text="Double-headed arrows join each basis element to its B coordinate vector below it"/>
                    <annotation ref="bottom-left" text="Bottom left: the coordinate vectors of 1, x, x squared, which are the standard basis columns of R 3: 1 0 0, then 0 1 0, then 0 0 1"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-bottom" text="An arrow labelled A 1 from the standard basis columns of R 3 to the columns on the right"/>
                    <annotation ref="bottom-right" text="Bottom right: the B 1 coordinate vectors of M 0, M 1, M 2, which are the first three standard basis columns of R 4: 1 0 0 0, then 0 1 0 0, then 0 0 1 0; these are the three columns of A 1"/>
                    <annotation ref="map-right" text="Double-headed arrows join each of M 0, M 1, M 2 to its B 1 coordinate vector below it"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Here is how the elements of <m>\mathcal{B}</m> are transformed:
          <md>
            S(1) = M_0, \qquad S(x) = M_1, \qquad S(x^2) = M_2,
          </md>
          which means, reading each image in the basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>,
          <md>
            A_1 = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{bmatrix}_{4 \times 3}.
          </md>
          To find <m>A_1</m> we had to express <m>S(1)</m>, <m>S(x)</m>, <m>S(x^2)</m> in
          terms of the basis elements <m>M_0, M_1, M_2</m>, and <m>M_3</m>. In other
          words, we needed to figure out how the basis elements of the domain transform
          with respect to the basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> of the codomain. The fourth
          element <m>M_3</m> of <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> is not the image of a basis
          element, which is why the last row of <m>A_1</m> is zero.
        </p>
        <p>
          After finding the output of the matrix transformation, we can use the
          isomorphism between <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> and <m>\R^4</m> and write the output
          as a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(6)</term> Write
          <m>M_{12} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <m>M_{21} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <m>M_{22} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and
          <m>M_{11} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, so that
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2 = \{ M_{12}, M_{21}, M_{22}, M_{11} \}</m>. Then
          <md>
            <mrow>
              S(1) \amp = M_0 = M_{12} - M_{21} + M_{22},
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              S(x) \amp = M_1 = M_{21} + M_{22} + M_{11},
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              S(x^2) \amp = M_2 = M_{12} + M_{11},
            </mrow>
          </md>
          and therefore
          <md>
            A_2 = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The picture is the one of <xref ref="fig-basis-diagram-S" text="type-global" />
          with a single change: <m>S</m> still sends <m>1, x, x^2</m> to
          <m>M_0, M_1, M_2</m>, but the images are now read in the basis
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-basis-diagram-S-b2">
          <caption>
            The action of <m>S</m> on the ordered basis <m>\mathcal{B}</m>, with the
            images read in the basis <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>, and the corresponding matrix
            <m>A_2</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-basis-diagram-S-b2" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>A square in which S carries 1, x, x squared to the matrices M0, M1, M2, while A2 carries the standard basis columns of R3 to the columns (1,-1,1,0), (0,1,1,1), (1,0,0,1).</shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pf-basis-diagram-S-b2">
              <diagram dimensions="(400,185)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="[0,0,400,185]">
                  <group at="square">
                    <group at="top-left">
                      <label anchor="(20,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(61,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>x</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(102,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>x^2</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="top-right">
                      <label anchor="(275,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>M_0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(327,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>M_1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(376,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>M_2</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="bottom-left">
                      <polygon points="((16,79),(12,79),(12,21),(16,21))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((24,79),(28,79),(28,21),(24,21))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(20,68)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(20,50)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(20,32)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((57,79),(53,79),(53,21),(57,21))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((65,79),(69,79),(69,21),(65,21))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(61,68)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(61,50)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(61,32)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((98,79),(94,79),(94,21),(98,21))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((106,79),(110,79),(110,21),(106,21))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(102,68)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(102,50)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(102,32)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>1</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="bottom-right">
                      <polygon points="((264,88),(260,88),(260,12),(264,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((286,88),(290,88),(290,12),(286,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(275,77)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(275,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>-1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(275,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(275,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((323,88),(319,88),(319,12),(323,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((331,88),(335,88),(335,12),(331,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(327,77)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(327,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(327,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(327,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>1</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((372,88),(368,88),(368,12),(372,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((380,88),(384,88),(384,12),(380,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(376,77)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(376,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(376,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(376,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>1</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="map-left">
                      <line endpoints="[(20,135),(20,85)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(61,135),(61,85)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(102,135),(102,85)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <label anchor="(117,114.5)" alignment="east" scale="0.8"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\mathcal{B}}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="map-right">
                      <line endpoints="[(275,135),(275,94)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(327,135),(327,94)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(376,135),(376,94)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <label anchor="(253,114.5)" alignment="west" scale="0.8"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\mathcal{B}_2}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-top">
                      <line endpoints="[(124,149),(246,149)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(182,155)" alignment="north" scale="1"><m>S</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-bottom">
                      <line endpoints="[(124,50),(246,50)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(182,56)" alignment="north" scale="1"><m>A_2</m></label>
                    </group>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="square" text="The same square as before, with the images now read in the basis B 2 of M 2 2">
                    <annotation ref="top-left" text="Top left: the three elements 1, x, x squared of the ordered basis B of P 2"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-top" text="An arrow labelled S from the basis elements to their images"/>
                    <annotation ref="top-right" text="Top right: their images under S, in the same order: the matrices M 0, M 1, M 2"/>
                    <annotation ref="map-left" text="Double-headed arrows join each basis element to its B coordinate vector below it"/>
                    <annotation ref="bottom-left" text="Bottom left: the standard basis columns of R 3: 1 0 0, then 0 1 0, then 0 0 1"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-bottom" text="An arrow labelled A 2 from the standard basis columns of R 3 to the columns on the right"/>
                    <annotation ref="bottom-right" text="Bottom right: the B 2 coordinate vectors of M 0, M 1, M 2: 1, negative 1, 1, 0, then 0 1 1 1, then 1 0 0 1; these are the three columns of A 2"/>
                    <annotation ref="map-right" text="Double-headed arrows join each of M 0, M 1, M 2 to its B 2 coordinate vector below it"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Alternatively, we can find a matrix that changes the basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>
          to <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>. From
          <md>
            M_0 = M_{12} - M_{21} + M_{22}, \quad
            M_1 = M_{21} + M_{22} + M_{11}, \quad
            M_2 = M_{12} + M_{11}, \quad
            M_3 = M_{11},
          </md>
          we get
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1} = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad \text{and} \qquad
            P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1} \, A_1 = A_2.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The reason is the familiar one: from
          <m>A_1 [\vec{u}]_{\mathcal{B}} = [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{B}_1}</m>, multiplying both
          sides by <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m> gives
          <m>A_2 [\vec{u}]_{\mathcal{B}} = [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{B}_2}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-trace-transformation">
      <title>The Trace as a Transformation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <term>trace</term> of a square matrix is the sum of its diagonal entries.
          On <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> it defines the map
          <m>\operatorname{tr} : \mathbb{M}_{22} \to \R</m>,
          <md>
            \operatorname{tr} \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix}
            = a + d,
          </md>
          where the codomain <m>\R</m> is itself a vector space, of dimension <m>1</m>,
          with ordered basis <m>\{ 1 \}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Show that <m>\operatorname{tr}</m> is a linear transformation.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Describe <m>\operatorname{Nul} (\operatorname{tr})</m> and find a basis
                for it. Is <m>\operatorname{tr}</m> one-to-one?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>What is the range of <m>\operatorname{tr}</m>? Is it onto?</p></li>
            <li><p>Is <m>\operatorname{tr}</m> an isomorphism?</p></li>
            <li><p>Verify the rank<ndash />nullity relation for <m>\operatorname{tr}</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Consider the ordered basis
                <m>\mathcal{B} = \{ M_{11}, M_{12}, M_{21}, M_{22} \}</m> of
                <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m>, where <m>M_{ij}</m> has a <m>1</m> in position
                <m>(i,j)</m> and zeros elsewhere, and the ordered basis <m>\{1\}</m> of
                <m>\R</m>. Find the matrix of the transformation
                <m>\operatorname{tr}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Use that matrix to compute
                <m>\operatorname{tr} \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp -3 \\ 7 \amp -9 \end{bmatrix}</m>
                through coordinates.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What is the matrix of <m>\operatorname{tr}</m> if the basis of the
                codomain is changed to <m>\{ 2 \}</m>?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(1)</term> For matrices
          <m>M = \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <m>N = \begin{bmatrix} a' \amp b' \\ c' \amp d' \end{bmatrix}</m> and a scalar
          <m>k</m>,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \operatorname{tr}(M + N) \amp = (a + a') + (d + d')
              = \operatorname{tr} M + \operatorname{tr} N,
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \operatorname{tr}(k M) \amp = ka + kd = k \operatorname{tr} M,
            </mrow>
          </md>
          so <m>\operatorname{tr}</m> respects addition and scalar multiplication and is
          a linear transformation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(2)</term> A matrix is in the null space exactly when its diagonal
          entries cancel:
          <md>
            \operatorname{Nul} (\operatorname{tr})
            = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp -a \end{bmatrix}
              \; : \; a, b, c \in \R \right\}
            = \operatorname{Span} \left\{
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} \right\}.
          </md>
          The three spanning matrices are linearly independent (they occupy different
          entries), so they form a basis and
          <m>\dim \operatorname{Nul} (\operatorname{tr}) = 3</m>. Since the null space
          contains nonzero vectors <mdash /> for instance
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix} \mapsto 0</m> <mdash />
          the transformation is NOT one-to-one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(3)</term> Every real number is a trace: for any <m>r \in \R</m>,
          <md>
            \operatorname{tr} \begin{bmatrix} r \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} = r.
          </md>
          Hence the range is all of <m>\R</m> and <m>\operatorname{tr}</m> is onto.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(4)</term> No. An isomorphism must be one-to-one and onto, and by (2)
          the trace is not one-to-one. (No transformation
          <m>\mathbb{M}_{22} \to \R</m> could be an isomorphism: the dimensions
          <m>4</m> and <m>1</m> do not match.)
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(5)</term> From (2) and (3),
          <md>
            \underbrace{\dim (\text{range of } \operatorname{tr})}_{1}
            + \underbrace{\dim \operatorname{Nul} (\operatorname{tr})}_{3}
            = 4 = \dim (\mathbb{M}_{22}),
          </md>
          as the rank<ndash />nullity relation predicts.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(6)</term> The strategy is the one of the previous example. The coordinate
          map of <m>\mathcal{B}</m> identifies <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> with <m>\R^4</m>, and
          the coordinate map of <m>\{1\}</m> identifies <m>\R</m> with <m>\R^1</m> (the
          number <m>r = r(1)</m> has the single coordinate <m>r</m>). So the trace
          corresponds to a matrix transformation <m>\R^4 \to \R^1</m>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-trace-square">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>\operatorname{tr} : \mathbb{M}_{22} \to \R</m> and the
            matrix transformation <m>\R^4 \to \R^1</m> induced by the coordinate maps.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-trace-square" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>A commutative square: the trace maps M22 to R across the top, a matrix A maps R4 to R1 across the bottom, and the coordinate maps of the two bases join top to bottom.</shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pf-trace-square">
              <diagram dimensions="(340,196)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="[0,0,340,196]">
                  <group at="square">
                    <label at="node-tl" anchor="(62,164)" alignment="center" scale="1.2"><m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m></label>
                    <label at="node-tr" anchor="(278,164)" alignment="center" scale="1.2"><m>\mathbb{R}</m></label>
                    <label at="node-bl" anchor="(62,36)" alignment="center" scale="1.2"><m>\mathbb{R}^4</m></label>
                    <label at="node-br" anchor="(278,36)" alignment="center" scale="1.2"><m>\mathbb{R}^1</m></label>
                    <group at="arrow-top">
                      <line endpoints="[(92,164),(242,164)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(167,170)" alignment="north" scale="1.1"><m>\operatorname{tr}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-left">
                      <line endpoints="[(62,144),(62,56)]" arrows="2" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(69,100)" alignment="east" scale="0.9"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\mathcal{B}}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-right">
                      <line endpoints="[(278,144),(278,56)]" arrows="2" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(271,100)" alignment="west" scale="0.9"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\{1\}}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-bottom">
                      <line endpoints="[(92,36),(248,36)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(170,42)" alignment="north" scale="1.1"><m>A</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(170,29)" alignment="south" scale="0.8"><m>\text{matrix transformation}</m></label>
                    </group>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="square" text="A commutative square relating the trace to a matrix transformation">
                    <annotation ref="arrow-top" text="Across the top, the trace maps the space M 2 2 of 2 by 2 matrices to the real numbers R"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-left" text="On the left, the coordinate map of the basis B identifies M 2 2 with R 4, in both directions"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-right" text="On the right, the coordinate map of the basis consisting of the number 1 identifies R with R 1, in both directions"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-bottom" text="Across the bottom, multiplication by the 1 by 4 matrix A maps R 4 to R 1"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          We transform the basis elements of the domain and read each
          output in the basis <m>\{1\}</m> of <m>\R</m>:
          <md>
            \operatorname{tr}(M_{11}) = 1, \quad
            \operatorname{tr}(M_{12}) = 0, \quad
            \operatorname{tr}(M_{21}) = 0, \quad
            \operatorname{tr}(M_{22}) = 1.
          </md>
          Each column of the matrix is the coordinate vector of one output, which here is
          a single number, so the matrix of the transformation is the
          <m>1 \times 4</m> row
          <md>
            A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          At the level of the ordered basis, the trace sends
          <m>M_{11}, M_{12}, M_{21}, M_{22}</m> to <m>1, 0, 0, 1</m>. In coordinates, the
          four standard basis columns of <m>\R^4</m> go to the four
          <m>1 \times 1</m> columns <m>[1], [0], [0], [1]</m>, which side by side are the
          matrix <m>A</m>:
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-trace-basis">
          <caption>
            The action of <m>\operatorname{tr}</m> on the ordered basis
            <m>\mathcal{B}</m> and the corresponding matrix <m>A</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-trace-basis" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>A square in which the trace carries M11, M12, M21, M22 to 1, 0, 0, 1, while A carries the four standard basis columns of R4 to the one-entry columns 1, 0, 0, 1.</shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pf-trace-basis">
              <diagram dimensions="(400,185)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="[0,0,400,185]">
                  <group at="square">
                    <group at="top-left">
                      <label anchor="(26,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>M_{11}</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(66,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>M_{12}</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(106,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>M_{21}</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(146,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4D4D4D"><m>M_{22}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="top-right">
                      <label anchor="(296,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(324,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(352,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(380,149)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4D4D4D"><m>1</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="bottom-left">
                      <polygon points="((22,88),(18,88),(18,12),(22,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((30,88),(34,88),(34,12),(30,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(26,77)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(26,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(26,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(26,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((62,88),(58,88),(58,12),(62,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((70,88),(74,88),(74,12),(70,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(66,77)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(66,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(66,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(66,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((102,88),(98,88),(98,12),(102,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((110,88),(114,88),(114,12),(110,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(106,77)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(106,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(106,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(106,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((142,88),(138,88),(138,12),(142,12))" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((150,88),(154,88),(154,12),(150,12))" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(146,77)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4D4D4D"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(146,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4D4D4D"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(146,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4D4D4D"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(146,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4D4D4D"><m>1</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="bottom-right">
                      <polygon points="((292,61),(288,61),(288,39),(292,39))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((300,61),(304,61),(304,39),(300,39))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(296,50)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((320,61),(316,61),(316,39),(320,39))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((328,61),(332,61),(332,39),(328,39))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(324,50)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((348,61),(344,61),(344,39),(348,39))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((356,61),(360,61),(360,39),(356,39))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(352,50)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((376,61),(372,61),(372,39),(376,39))" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((384,61),(388,61),(388,39),(384,39))" stroke="#4D4D4D" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(380,50)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4D4D4D"><m>1</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="map-left">
                      <line endpoints="[(26,135),(26,94)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(66,135),(66,94)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(106,135),(106,94)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(146,135),(146,94)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <label anchor="(167,114.5)" alignment="east" scale="0.8"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\mathcal{B}}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="map-right">
                      <line endpoints="[(296,135),(296,67)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(324,135),(324,67)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(352,135),(352,67)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(380,135),(380,67)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <label anchor="(281,114.5)" alignment="west" scale="0.8"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\{1\}}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-top">
                      <line endpoints="[(174,149),(274,149)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(221,155)" alignment="north" scale="1"><m>\operatorname{tr}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-bottom">
                      <line endpoints="[(174,50),(274,50)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(221,56)" alignment="north" scale="1"><m>A</m></label>
                    </group>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="square" text="A square showing the trace of each element of the basis B and, underneath, the same thing in coordinates">
                    <annotation ref="top-left" text="Top left: the four elements M 1 1, M 1 2, M 2 1, M 2 2 of the ordered basis B of M 2 2"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-top" text="An arrow labelled t r from the basis elements to their traces"/>
                    <annotation ref="top-right" text="Top right: their traces, in the same order: 1, 0, 0, 1"/>
                    <annotation ref="map-left" text="Double-headed arrows join each basis element to its B coordinate vector below it"/>
                    <annotation ref="bottom-left" text="Bottom left: the coordinate vectors of the basis elements, which are the four standard basis columns of R 4"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-bottom" text="An arrow labelled A from the standard basis columns of R 4 to the entries on the right"/>
                    <annotation ref="bottom-right" text="Bottom right: the coordinates of the four traces, each a single number: 1, 0, 0, 1; side by side they are the four columns of the 1 by 4 matrix A"/>
                    <annotation ref="map-right" text="Double-headed arrows join each trace to its coordinate below it"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The corresponding matrix transformation <m>\R^4 \to \R</m> tells the same
          story as (2) and (3) in the language of pivots: <m>A</m> has a pivot in its
          only row, so it is onto, but it cannot have a pivot in every one of its four
          columns, so it is not one-to-one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(7)</term> In the basis <m>\mathcal{B}</m>,
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp -3 \\ 7 \amp -9 \end{bmatrix} \right]_{\mathcal{B}}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ -3 \\ 7 \\ -9 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            A \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ -3 \\ 7 \\ -9 \end{bmatrix}
            = 4 - 9 = -5,
          </md>
          which agrees with computing the trace directly: <m>4 + (-9) = -5</m>.
          This is the path down, across and up in
          <xref ref="fig-trace-square" text="type-global" />: take coordinates,
          multiply by <m>A</m>, and read the result as a real number.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(8)</term> With respect to the basis <m>\{2\}</m> of <m>\R</m>, a real
          number <m>r</m> has coordinate <m>r/2</m>, since <m>r = \tfrac{r}{2}(2)</m>.
          Each entry of the matrix is now the <m>\{2\}</m>-coordinate of the
          corresponding output:
          <md>
            A' = \begin{bmatrix} \tfrac{1}{2} \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \tfrac{1}{2} \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          As in the previous example, changing a basis of the codomain rescales the rows
          of the matrix without changing the transformation itself.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-p2-change-of-basis">
      <title>A Change of Basis in <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the standard ordered basis
          <m>\mathcal{B}_1 = \{ 1, x, x^2 \}</m> of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> and the set
          <md>
            \mathcal{B}_2 = \{ 1, \; 1 + x, \; 1 + x + x^2 \}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Show that <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> is a basis of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the change-of-basis matrix
                <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m>, which converts
                <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>-coordinates into <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>-coordinates.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the coordinates of <m>q(x) = 3 + 2x + 4x^2</m> relative to the
                basis <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(1)</term> Since <m>\dim \mathbb{P}_2 = 3</m>, it is enough to show
          that the three polynomials are linearly independent. Suppose
          <md>
            a(1) + b(1 + x) + c(1 + x + x^2) = 0,
          </md>
          that is,
          <md>
            (a + b + c) + (b + c)x + c x^2 = 0.
          </md>
          Comparing coefficients gives <m>c = 0</m>, then <m>b + c = 0</m> forces
          <m>b = 0</m>, and then <m>a + b + c = 0</m> forces <m>a = 0</m>. Only the
          trivial combination produces the zero polynomial, so <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> is
          linearly independent and hence a basis of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(2)</term> The easy direction is the other one: each element of
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> is already written in terms of <m>\{1, x, x^2\}</m>, so
          reading off coordinates columnwise,
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}_1 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_2}
            = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          The matrix we want is its inverse,
          <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}
          = \left( P_{\mathcal{B}_1 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_2} \right)^{-1}</m>, which we
          can also compute directly by expressing each element of <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>
          in the basis <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>:
          <md>
            1 = (1), \qquad
            x = (1 + x) - (1), \qquad
            x^2 = (1 + x + x^2) - (1 + x),
          </md>
          so that
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}
            = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          In the language of this section,
          <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m> is the matrix of the
          identity transformation
          <m>\operatorname{id} : \mathbb{P}_2 \to \mathbb{P}_2</m> with respect to the
          basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> of the domain and the basis <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>
          of the codomain: the transformation does nothing, and the matrix only
          translates between the two coordinate systems.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-change-of-basis-square">
          <caption>
            The identity transformation of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> and the matrix
            transformation <m>\R^3 \to \R^3</m> induced by the coordinate maps of
            <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> and <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-change-of-basis-square" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>A commutative square: the identity maps P2 to P2 across the top, the change of basis matrix maps R3 to R3 across the bottom, and the coordinate maps of B1 and B2 join top to bottom.</shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pf-change-of-basis-square">
              <diagram dimensions="(340,196)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="[0,0,340,196]">
                  <group at="square">
                    <label at="node-tl" anchor="(62,164)" alignment="center" scale="1.2"><m>\mathbb{P}_2</m></label>
                    <label at="node-tr" anchor="(278,164)" alignment="center" scale="1.2"><m>\mathbb{P}_2</m></label>
                    <label at="node-bl" anchor="(62,36)" alignment="center" scale="1.2"><m>\mathbb{R}^3</m></label>
                    <label at="node-br" anchor="(278,36)" alignment="center" scale="1.2"><m>\mathbb{R}^3</m></label>
                    <group at="arrow-top">
                      <line endpoints="[(92,164),(242,164)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(167,170)" alignment="north" scale="1.1"><m>\operatorname{id}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-left">
                      <line endpoints="[(62,144),(62,56)]" arrows="2" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(69,100)" alignment="east" scale="0.9"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\mathcal{B}_1}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-right">
                      <line endpoints="[(278,144),(278,56)]" arrows="2" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(271,100)" alignment="west" scale="0.9"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\mathcal{B}_2}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-bottom">
                      <line endpoints="[(92,36),(248,36)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(170,42)" alignment="north" scale="1.1"><m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(170,29)" alignment="south" scale="0.8"><m>\text{matrix transformation}</m></label>
                    </group>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="square" text="A commutative square relating the identity transformation of P 2 to a change of basis matrix">
                    <annotation ref="arrow-top" text="Across the top, the identity transformation maps P 2 to itself and changes nothing"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-left" text="On the left, the coordinate map of the basis B 1 identifies P 2 with R 3, in both directions"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-right" text="On the right, the coordinate map of the basis B 2 identifies P 2 with R 3, in both directions"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-bottom" text="Across the bottom, multiplication by the change of basis matrix from B 1 to B 2 maps R 3 to R 3"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          At the level of the ordered basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>, the identity leaves
          <m>1, x, x^2</m> where they are, but their coordinates are taken in
          <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> on the left and in <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> on the right. The
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>-coordinate vectors of <m>1, x, x^2</m> are the columns of
          <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m>:
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-change-of-basis-basis">
          <caption>
            The identity acting on the ordered basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> and the
            corresponding matrix <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-change-of-basis-basis" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>A square in which the identity carries 1, x, x squared to themselves, while the change of basis matrix carries the standard basis columns of R3 to the columns (1,0,0), (-1,1,0), (0,-1,1).</shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pf-change-of-basis-basis">
              <diagram dimensions="(402,167)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="[0,0,402,167]">
                  <group at="square">
                    <group at="top-left">
                      <label anchor="(20,131)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(62,131)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>x</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(104,131)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>x^2</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="top-right">
                      <label anchor="(270,131)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(319,131)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>x</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(375,131)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>x^2</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="bottom-left">
                      <polygon points="((16,70),(12,70),(12,12),(16,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((24,70),(28,70),(28,12),(24,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(20,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(20,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(20,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((58,70),(54,70),(54,12),(58,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((66,70),(70,70),(70,12),(66,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(62,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(62,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(62,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((100,70),(96,70),(96,12),(100,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((108,70),(112,70),(112,12),(108,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(104,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(104,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(104,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>1</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="bottom-right">
                      <polygon points="((266,70),(262,70),(262,12),(266,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((274,70),(278,70),(278,12),(274,12))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(270,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(270,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(270,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#5E011C"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((308,70),(304,70),(304,12),(308,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((330,70),(334,70),(334,12),(330,12))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(319,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>-1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(319,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(319,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#4A5D23"><m>0</m></label>
                      <polygon points="((364,70),(360,70),(360,12),(364,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <polygon points="((386,70),(390,70),(390,12),(386,12))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(375,59)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>0</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(375,41)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>-1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(375,23)" alignment="center" scale="0.8" color="#876801"><m>1</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="map-left">
                      <line endpoints="[(20,117),(20,76)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(62,117),(62,76)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(104,117),(104,76)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <label anchor="(119,96.5)" alignment="east" scale="0.8"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\mathcal{B}_1}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="map-right">
                      <line endpoints="[(270,117),(270,76)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(319,117),(319,76)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <line endpoints="[(375,117),(375,76)]" arrows="2" stroke="#6B6B6B" thickness="1"/>
                      <label anchor="(255,96.5)" alignment="west" scale="0.8"><m>[\,\cdot\,]_{\mathcal{B}_2}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-top">
                      <line endpoints="[(126,131),(248,131)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(184,137)" alignment="north" scale="1"><m>\operatorname{id}</m></label>
                    </group>
                    <group at="arrow-bottom">
                      <line endpoints="[(126,41),(248,41)]" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                      <label anchor="(184,47)" alignment="north" scale="1"><m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m></label>
                    </group>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="square" text="A square showing that the identity leaves each element of the basis B 1 alone while its coordinates change from B 1 to B 2">
                    <annotation ref="top-left" text="Top left: the three elements 1, x, x squared of the basis B 1"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-top" text="An arrow labelled i d, the identity transformation"/>
                    <annotation ref="top-right" text="Top right: the same three polynomials 1, x, x squared"/>
                    <annotation ref="map-left" text="Double-headed arrows join each polynomial to its B 1 coordinate vector below it"/>
                    <annotation ref="bottom-left" text="Bottom left: the B 1 coordinate vectors of 1, x, x squared, which are the standard basis columns 1 0 0, then 0 1 0, then 0 0 1"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-bottom" text="An arrow labelled with the change of basis matrix from B 1 to B 2"/>
                    <annotation ref="bottom-right" text="Bottom right: the B 2 coordinate vectors of 1, x, x squared: 1 0 0, then negative 1, 1, 0, then 0, negative 1, 1; these are the three columns of the change of basis matrix"/>
                    <annotation ref="map-right" text="Double-headed arrows join each polynomial to its B 2 coordinate vector below it"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          <term>(3)</term> Relative to <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> the coordinates of
          <m>q</m> are read off directly, and the matrix converts them:
          <md>
            [q(x)]_{\mathcal{B}_1} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            [q(x)]_{\mathcal{B}_2}
            = P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1} [q(x)]_{\mathcal{B}_1}
            = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -2 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          As a check,
          <md>
            1(1) - 2(1 + x) + 4(1 + x + x^2)
            = (1 - 2 + 4) + (-2 + 4)x + 4x^2
            = 3 + 2x + 4x^2 = q(x).
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
